Q: Why is an Atomic Struct of Two 64-bit Variables not Lock-Free on X86? A: <p>A lock-free 16-byte atomic compare-exchange on x66 is one of the most inconsistently-implemented parts of the atomic library.  This is supposed to use the <a href="https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/cmpxchg8b:cmpxchg16b" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>LOCK CMPXCHG16B</code></a> instruction.  Naturally-aligned loads and stores of a 128-bit AVX register with a 16-byte-aligned address are guaranteed to be atomic on newer x86 CPUs (although the precise details vary by manufacturer).  Unaligned 16-byte objects might overlap two different cache lines and need to lock the bus to run atomically.  LLVM generates inline lock-free atomic code for this and report it always lock-free.  GCC can select lock-free implementations at runtime through <code>libatomic</code>, but reports its implementation as not-lock-free.  MSVC does not (as of 19.44) generate lock-free instructions, but does make them available through an intrinsic.</p>
<p>On LLVM compilers (tested Clang 21.1 and ICX 2025.3), the following assertion succeeds:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct /* alignas(16) */ Foo {
     std::uint64_t tick = 0;
     std::uint64_t tock = 0;
};

static_assert(std::atomic&lt;Foo&gt;::is_always_lock_free);
</code></pre>
<p>However, some operations will compile differently on both compilers, depending on whether the <code>alignas(16)</code> specifier is commented out or not.  For exmaple:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>atomic.compare_exchange_strong(
    foo,
    Foo{},
    std::memory_order_relaxed,
    std::memory_order_relaxed );
</code></pre>
<p>When <code>Foo</code> is declared <code>alignas(16)</code>, LLVM compilers will generate an inline <code>lock cmpxchg16b</code> instruction for this.  Without any alignment specifier, LLVM compilers will make a function call.  They implement <code>std::atomic&lt;Foo&gt;::load</code> and <code>std::atomic&lt;Foo&gt;::store</code> with AVX aligned load/store instructions.</p>
<p>GCC has a <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/x86-Options.html#index-mprefer-vector-width" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>=mcx16</code> flag</a> that the documentation says will “generate CMPXCHG16B instructions in 64-bit code to implement compare-and-exchange operations on 16-byte aligned 128-bit objects.”  However, even with it active, GCC 15.2 generates <code>call</code> instructions to <code>libatomic</code> for its atomic operations.</p>
<p>GCC has reported <code>false</code> for both <code>std::atomic::is_always_lock_free</code> and <code>std::atomic::is_lock_free()</code> on a 16-byte type for x86_64 since version 7.  In version 6, <code>std::atomic::is_lock_free()</code> would report <code>true</code> at runtime, even when compiled with <code>-march=nocona</code>, which does not have <code>CMPXCHG16B</code>  (presumably because it is linked to a version of <code>libatomic</code> that knows it’s running on a modern CPU). When C++17 added <code>std::atomic&lt;T&gt;::is_always_lock_free</code> to C++17, the only officially atomic instruction was <code>lock cmpxchg</code>.  <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/61994385/4474419">Allegedly,</a> the implementers deemed a compare-exchange loop that locks the bus at the hardware level and could fail an arbitrary number of times only technically avoided a software lock, and did not really behave like programmers would expect from a lock-free atomic load or store.  (Peter Cordes brought up that an atomic load implemented with <code>cmpxchg16b</code> will fail on read-only memory, although an implementation could place atomic variables only in writable data segments.)</p>
<p>MSVC 19.44 with <code>/arch:AVX /O2</code> will generate code that uses a mutex lock for <code>std::atomic</code> 16-byte types.  However, it provides an <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/intrinsics/interlockedcompareexchange128?view=msvc-170" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>_InterlockedCompareExchange128</code></a> intrinsic function you can use.</p>
<p>Try it yourself on <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/MoMoaT9z3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the Godbolt Compiler Explorer.</a></p>

Q: Arithmetic inside a for loop batch file A: <p>Is <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/995714/phuclv">phuclv</a> right?  Let's find out:</p>
<pre><code>setlocal EnableDelayedExpansion
set bornyesterday=0
for /L %%i in (%bornyesterday%,1,1) do (
    set u=%%i
    set /a true=%%i+9
    set /a alsotrue=!u!+9
    set /a false=i+9
    echo !true! !alsotrue! !false!
)
echo output will be: 
echo        9 9 9
echo        10 10 9
echo (note: in the first cycle %false% is in fact 9 making it true; 
echo but in the 2nd cycle it is still 9, proving that:
echo i was not %bornyesterday% but u was. were? was?)
</code></pre>
<p>Now we know. Happy birthday,  <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/995714/phuclv">phuclv</a>!</p>
